import Foundation

struct GuideSection: Identifiable { let id = UUID(); let title: String; let colour: String; let body: String }
struct Release: Identifiable { let id = UUID(); let version: String; let date: String; let items: [String] }

enum Guide {
    static var version: String {
        let v = Bundle.main.infoDictionary?["CFBundleShortVersionString"] as? String ?? "?"
        let b = Bundle.main.infoDictionary?["CFBundleVersion"] as? String ?? "?"
        return "\(v) (\(b))"
    }

    static let howItWorks: [GuideSection] = [
        GuideSection(title: "What this app is for", colour: "purple", body: "AMS Shortcut Overview helps you learn keyboard shortcuts. It puts one colour card per app on your desktop, along the bottom of the screen (in a second row above if they do not fit), behind all your windows. Each card lists the shortcuts you are still learning. You see them every time you look at the desktop, so they stick. It works on the Mac only, has no Dock icon, and nothing leaves your Mac."),
        GuideSection(title: "The cards on the desktop", colour: "coral", body: "Every app has its own card in its own colour: the app's name at the top, then one row per shortcut. On the left of each row is a small circle, in the middle what the shortcut does, and on the right the keys to press, drawn as little keys. ⌘ is Command, ⌥ is Option, ⌃ is Control and ⇧ is Shift.\n\nA card shows up to 6 shortcuts. If there are more, it says \"+2 more\" at the bottom; the next ones move up as you learn the first. The number of rows can be changed in Settings. The top right of a card says how many of that app's shortcuts you have learned.\n\nA new installation starts with 5 cards: Obsidian, Raycast, Fusion, Finder and Xcode, each with 8 shortcuts. You can add more ready-made apps or remove cards at any time (see \"Ready-made apps\").\n\nIf the cards do not fit in one row, the extra ones go into a second row above the first (and a third, if needed). The first cards stay where they are on the bottom row; the newest apps go on top. Each card is 260 points wide, so 6 fit in a row on a 16-inch MacBook Pro; a smaller screen fits fewer. The number changes by itself when you connect or remove a screen.\n\nThe cards sit at desktop level: windows always cover them. To see them, move your windows aside or show the desktop (for example by clicking the wallpaper, or with a spread of thumb and three fingers on the trackpad)."),
        GuideSection(title: "Ticking a shortcut off", colour: "teal", body: "When you know a shortcut, click its circle. A tick appears, the row is crossed out, and after a moment it disappears from the card. You can also right-click a row and choose \"I know it\".\n\nTwo weeks later the shortcut comes back once, at the top of its card, with a small \"Recheck\" label. If you still know it, tick it again: it leaves the card and there are no more rechecks. If you have forgotten it, right-click it and choose \"Still learning\": it stays on the card as before.\n\nTicking never deletes a shortcut. It is only hidden from the card. It stays in your list: you see it on the Shortcuts page and on the Learned page, and \"Bring back\" puts it on its card again at any time. Nothing is ever deleted. \"Remove\" (in the shortcut's window, or for a whole app) only moves things to the Removed page (see \"Removed — nothing is ever deleted\").\n\nWhen every shortcut of an app is learned, its card is hidden from the desktop. The app and its shortcuts stay in your list."),
        GuideSection(title: "Removed — nothing is ever deleted", colour: "coral", body: "Nothing is ever deleted. \"Remove\" only hides: it moves a shortcut, or a whole app with its card, to the Removed page in the window.\n\n• In the shortcut's window, the button is called \"Remove\". Next to an app's name on the Shortcuts page there is a \"Remove\" button too. It asks first: \"Remove Finder from the desktop?\" with the note \"The card and its 8 shortcuts move to the Removed page. Bring them back from there any time.\"\n• The Removed page starts with the line \"Nothing is ever deleted. Bring back what you want to see again.\" Removed apps are listed with their shortcuts and one button, \"Bring back app\". Single removed shortcuts are listed under their app's name, each with \"Bring back\".\n• Something you bring back returns exactly as it was: learned or not, the same recheck date, the same colour. If another app took that colour meanwhile, it gets the next free one.\n• If nothing is removed, the page says \"Nothing removed\".\n• \"Add ready-made app\": if you removed a ready-made app earlier, choosing it there brings your own one back as it was, instead of adding a second copy. Typing a removed app's name when you add a shortcut also brings that app's card back.\n\nEverything you remove can be brought back."),
        GuideSection(title: "Learned this week", colour: "green", body: "A small count shows how many shortcuts you have learned this week, and how many weeks in a row you learned something, so you can see your progress.\n\n• In the menu of the key icon in the menu bar, a grey line (you cannot click it) says \"Learned this week: 3\" (for example). From 2 weeks in a row it says \"Learned this week: 3 · 2 weeks in a row\". It sits just above the Version line.\n• At the top of the Learned page in the window, a line says \"This week: 3 learned · 12 learned in all\", and from 2 weeks in a row \"This week: 3 learned · 12 learned in all · 2 weeks in a row\". The second number is everything that is on the Learned page right now.\n\nWeeks in a row: the app counts how many weeks in a row you learned at least one shortcut. A week is Monday to Sunday. The current week does not break the run while it still has no tick: the run only ends when a full week passes with nothing learned.\n\nWhat counts: a shortcut counts for the week in which you first ticked it as learned. The week starts on Monday (the Swedish calendar) and the count starts again at 0 on the next Monday. It does not matter what happened afterwards: if you tick it again at the recheck, it still counts for its first week. If you press \"Bring back\" (or \"Still learning\"), the learned date is cleared and the shortcut no longer counts.")
,
        GuideSection(title: "Adding and changing shortcuts", colour: "blue", body: "Three ways to add one:\n1. Click the small + at the top right of a card: the new shortcut goes to that app.\n2. Click the key icon in the menu bar and choose \"Add shortcut…\".\n3. In the window, press \"Add shortcut\" at the bottom of the Shortcuts page.\n\nFill in the app, what the shortcut does, and the keys. Type the keys, or press the ⌘ ⌥ ⌃ ⇧ buttons to add a symbol, then type the letter. \"Looks like\" shows how it will look on the card. Save, or Cancel (Escape also cancels).\n\nType a new app name and the app gets a new card with the next free colour. To change or remove a shortcut, right-click it on its card and choose \"Change…\", or press \"Change\" next to it on the Shortcuts page. The Remove button is in the same window. It only moves the shortcut to the Removed page."),
        GuideSection(title: "Ready-made apps", colour: "blue", body: "The app carries ready-made lists of 8 checked shortcuts each for 8 apps: Obsidian (purple), Raycast (coral), Fusion (teal), Finder (blue), Xcode (amber), Safari (green), Mail (pink) and Notes (amber). All were checked against the makers' own pages; Mail and Notes against Apple's own pages.\n\nWhat is on your desktop:\n• A new installation starts with Obsidian, Raycast, Fusion, Finder and Xcode (5 cards, 40 shortcuts). Safari, Mail and Notes are not added by themselves.\n• If you already used an earlier version, Finder and Xcode were added once, the first time version 0.3 started, and only if no app of that name was on your list yet.\n\nAdd one yourself: on the Shortcuts page press \"Add ready-made app\" at the bottom. A menu lists the ready-made apps that are not on your list yet. Pick one and its card appears with its 8 shortcuts, in its own colour, or in the next free colour if that colour is already taken by another app. When all 8 are on your list, the button is greyed out.\n\nIf you removed a ready-made app earlier, choosing it there brings your own one back exactly as it was (what you learned, the recheck dates, your own shortcuts) instead of adding a second copy.\n\nRemove an app: on the Shortcuts page, each app's heading has a \"Remove\" button next to \"Move left\" and \"Move right\". It asks first: \"Remove Finder from the desktop?\" with the note \"The card and its 8 shortcuts move to the Removed page. Bring them back from there any time.\" Nothing is ever deleted: the app moves to the Removed page, with everything you learned, and \"Bring back app\" there puts it on the desktop again."),
        GuideSection(title: "The window", colour: "pink", body: "Click the key icon in the menu bar and choose \"Shortcuts and settings…\". The window has six pages:\n• Shortcuts: every shortcut of every app, with its state (empty = still learning, Recheck, Learned). Click the coloured dot next to an app's name to change its colour: every click tries the next of 7 colours; \"Move left\" and \"Move right\" change the order of the cards on the desktop, and \"Remove\" moves the app and its shortcuts to the Removed page after asking. At the bottom are the buttons \"Add shortcut\" and \"Add ready-made app\" (see \"Ready-made apps\").\n• Learned: a line at the top says \"This week: N learned · M learned in all\" (from 2 weeks in a row it adds \"· W weeks in a row\"); below it, everything you have learned, with the date of its recheck, or \"Learned, no more rechecks\". \"Bring back\" puts a shortcut back on its card.\n• Removed: everything you removed, with \"Bring back\" and \"Bring back app\". Nothing is ever deleted.\n• Settings: show or hide the cards, shortcuts per card (3 to 12), the two learning switches \"Today's shortcuts\" and \"Quiz me\" (both off until you switch them on), open at login, where your list is kept (with \"Show in Finder\"), \"A copy to keep anywhere\" (with \"Export my list…\", see \"Your list is safe\"), and the version.\n• How it works: this guide.\n• What's new: every version, newest first.\n\nThe menu of the keyboard key icon in the menu bar has the same two switches. \"Today's shortcuts\" and \"Quiz me\" each show a tick when they are on, next to \"Show cards on the desktop\". Just above the Version line the menu also has a grey line, \"Learned this week: N\" (with \"· W weeks in a row\" from 2 weeks), that you cannot click. If your list could not be saved, a red line \"Could not save your list\" also shows there. Switch them on or off in the menu or in Settings: both places always show the same state, and the cards change straight away."),
        GuideSection(title: "Finding the menu bar icon", colour: "teal", body: "The app's icon is a small keyboard key in the menu bar at the top right. It has its own place there, between AMS Mic Check and AMS Deck Mate, on the side that AMS Tidy Bar always shows, so it stays visible next to the clock even on a full bar with the camera notch. To move it, hold the ⌘ key and drag it sideways; the app remembers the new place. If you drag it to the left of Tidy Bar's thin line, Tidy Bar hides it in the drawer."),
        GuideSection(title: "Today's shortcuts", colour: "green", body: "Normally a card shows your shortcuts in the order of your list, so the same ones are always on top. With \"Today's shortcuts\" on, each card shows a fresh pick every day instead.\n\nHow it works:\n• Once a day the order of an app's shortcuts is shuffled. It stays the same all day, and a new order appears after midnight. The app checks the clock every hour and when your Mac wakes up, so the new pick is there when you sit down, even if the Mac slept through midnight.\n• Rechecks still come first, with their \"Recheck\" label, before today's pick.\n• The card shows as many shortcuts as \"Shortcuts per card\" says (3 to 12).\n• When you tick one off, the next of today's pick moves up to fill the place.\n• While the switch is on, the card's header says \"Today\" next to the app's name, so you know you are looking at the day's pick.\n\nThe shuffle is the same for the same day, so closing and opening the app does not change the pick. Only the next day does. Nothing about your list changes: the order on the Shortcuts page stays as you made it, and switching \"Today's shortcuts\" off brings the list order back at once.\n\nSwitch it on in Settings (\"Today's shortcuts\") or in the menu bar icon's menu. It is off by default."),
        GuideSection(title: "Quiz me", colour: "amber", body: "\"Quiz me\" turns the cards into a small test. With it on, the keys on the cards are hidden: each key shows as a blank key cap with a \"?\" on it. You still see how many keys the shortcut takes, but not which ones.\n\nTry to remember the keys first, then look:\n• Point at a row with the mouse, and the real keys appear. Move the mouse away and they hide again.\n• Or click the hidden keys: the real keys stay shown until you click them again. Handy if you want to look in peace.\n\nYou can still tick a shortcut off, right-click it and change it as usual: only the keys are hidden. A good way to use it: say the keys out loud (or press them) before you point at the row, then check.\n\n\"Quiz me\" works together with \"Today's shortcuts\": switch both on and every day you get a new pick to quiz yourself on. Switch it on in Settings (\"Quiz me\") or in the menu bar icon's menu. It is off by default."),
        GuideSection(title: "Your list is safe", colour: "amber", body: "Your shortcuts are kept in a file on this Mac (Settings, \"Show in Finder\"). The app protects it in several ways.\n\n• Safety copy: before every change the app keeps a copy of the previous list next to it.\n• One copy per day: in a folder \"Safety copies\" next to your list, the app keeps one dated copy per day, the list as it was before that day's first change. File names look like shortcuts-2026-11-16.json. Copies are kept for 30 days; older ones are cleared away.\n• A damaged list: if the list file cannot be read, or reads as \"no apps at all\" (the app only ever hides things, so a real list is never empty), the app puts that file aside (shortcuts-unreadable-….json, never overwritten) and starts from the safety copy. If that is no good, it uses the newest good dated copy. The window then shows an amber line saying where your list was restored from. The app also refuses to save an empty list over a good one.\n• If saving fails, a red line \"Could not save your list\" appears at the top of the window and in the menu bar menu. The app tries again every hour and with your next change, and the line goes away when it worked.\n• A copy to keep anywhere: on the Settings page, under \"Your list is kept in\", the row \"A copy to keep anywhere\" has the button \"Export my list…\". It opens the usual Save window, titled \"Export my list\", with a name filled in for you, for example \"AMS Shortcut Overview list 2026-11-16.json\" (today's date). You choose the place: iCloud Drive, Dropbox, a USB stick, anywhere. Cancel closes the window and nothing is written.\n• The file holds everything: every app and every shortcut, what you have learned, the recheck dates, the colours, and the removed ones too.\n• After a save, a grey line under the row says, for example, \"Last export: 16 Nov 2026 at 14:05 · 5 apps, 40 shortcuts\" (removed ones are counted). The line stays after a restart. If the file could not be written, a red line \"Could not export: …\" says why.\n• Exporting changes nothing in your list, and you can do it as often as you like. If you export twice to the same name, the Mac asks before it replaces the file.\n• The app cannot read an export back in by itself yet. The file is your own copy for now; putting it back is a job for a later version.\n\nThe first time it starts, the app fills in 8 shortcuts each for Obsidian, Raycast, Fusion, Finder and Xcode. Nothing is ever deleted: removed apps and shortcuts wait on the Removed page."),
    ]

    static let releases: [Release] = [
        Release(version: "0.5.1 (8)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "Checked with real clicks. Before, the screens were only checked from pictures. Now the app was tried with real clicks and mouse presses, on a separate test list (your real list was not touched).",
            "Tried and working: a tick on a desktop card, once as a button press and once as a real mouse press. The row is crossed out and leaves the card, the card counts \"1 learned\" and then \"2 learned\", and the list file holds it.",
            "Tried and working: Quiz me. A click on the \"?\" keys of a row shows the real keys for that row only.",
            "Tried and working: on the Shortcuts page, \"Remove\" next to an app asks \"Remove Obsidian from the desktop?\", and \"Remove\" moves the card with its 8 shortcuts to the Removed page. On the Removed page, \"Bring back app\" puts the card back as it was: same place, \"1 learned\" still there.",
            "Tried and working: in Settings, \"Export my list…\" opens the Save window with the name filled in, and Cancel writes nothing.",
            "Tried and working: the + on a card opens the editor with the app filled in. The ⌘ button adds the key, \"Looks like\" shows it at once, and Save adds the shortcut as a 9th row of that app.",
            "Tried and working: the first change of the day made the dated safety copy and the safety copy, as designed.",
            "Not tried yet: showing the keys by pointing with the mouse (only the click was tried), typing letters into the Keys field (the test tool could not type there), and the change at midnight.",
            "Fixed: the time of a tick. Before, when you ticked a shortcut, the app noted the time of its last hourly clock check instead of the moment of your click, up to one hour too early (in the check, two ticks made a minute apart both got the app's start time). The same happened when you removed something. Now the exact moment of your click is noted.",
            "What this changes for you: the recheck comes 14 days after the real moment of your tick, and a tick just after Monday midnight is counted in the right week.",
            "Seen with the window in front: in the question \"Remove … from the desktop?\" the button \"Remove\" is the coloured main button and the Return key presses it; Escape is Cancel. (While another app is in front, the Mac draws both buttons grey.)",
            "Tests: still 22, all passing. Nothing else changed.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.5 (7)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "New: \"Export my list\", one file you can keep anywhere. Before, your list lived only in the app's own folder on this Mac, plus the safety copies next to it. Now you can save one more copy wherever you like: iCloud Drive, Dropbox, a USB stick, anywhere.",
            "The Settings page has a new row, \"A copy to keep anywhere\", with the button \"Export my list…\". It sits directly under \"Your list is kept in\" and its \"Show in Finder\" button (before: the page had no export, so 1 row about your list, now 2).",
            "The button opens the usual Save window, titled \"Export my list\". The name is filled in for you, for example \"AMS Shortcut Overview list 2026-11-16.json\" (today's date). You choose the place. Cancel closes the window and nothing is written.",
            "The file holds everything: every app and every shortcut, what you have learned, the recheck dates, the colours, and the removed ones too. It has the same form as the app's own list file.",
            "After a save, a grey line under the row says, for example, \"Last export: 16 Nov 2026 at 14:05 · 5 apps, 40 shortcuts\". The counts include removed ones. The line stays after a restart. If the file could not be written, a red line \"Could not export: …\" says why.",
            "Exporting changes nothing in your list, and you can do it as often as you like. If you export twice to the same name, the Mac asks, as usual, before it replaces the file.",
            "The app cannot read an export back in by itself yet. The file is your own copy for now; putting it back is a job for a later version.",
            "More tests: 22 tests (before: 21). The new one checks that an exported file reads back exactly as the list was, with the learned and removed ones included.",
            "The How it works guide is updated in \"The window\" (the Settings page) and in \"Your list is safe\".",
            "Nothing else changed: the learning rules, the cards, \"Today's shortcuts\", \"Quiz me\" and your settings work as before.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.4 (6)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "New: a Removed page. Before, \"Delete\" in the shortcut window and \"Remove\" next to an app on the Shortcuts page took shortcuts away completely, and only the one safety copy still held them. Now nothing is ever deleted. The button in the shortcut window is called \"Remove\", and it and the app's \"Remove\" only hide: the shortcut, or the whole app with its card, moves to the new Removed page.",
            "Removing an app asks first, with new words. Before: \"Remove Finder and its 8 shortcuts?\". Now: \"Remove Finder from the desktop?\" with the note \"The card and its 8 shortcuts move to the Removed page. Bring them back from there any time.\"",
            "The window now has 6 pages (before: 5), in this order: Shortcuts, Learned, Removed, Settings, How it works, What's new.",
            "The Removed page starts with the line \"Nothing is ever deleted. Bring back what you want to see again.\" Removed apps are listed with their shortcuts and one button, \"Bring back app\". Single removed shortcuts are listed under their app's name, each with \"Bring back\". An empty page says \"Nothing removed\".",
            "Something you bring back returns exactly as it was: learned or not, the same recheck date, the same colour. If another app took the colour meanwhile, it gets the next free one.",
            "\"Add ready-made app\": if you removed a ready-made app earlier, choosing it there now brings your own one back as it was. Before, it was added again fresh, with nothing learned. Typing a removed app's name when you add a shortcut also brings that app's card back.",
            "Everything you remove can now be brought back. Before, two buttons took shortcuts away with no way back in the app.",
            "A second row of cards. Before, all cards sat in one row along the bottom, and when there were more than the screen is wide, the outer ones were cut off at both edges (on a 16-inch MacBook Pro 6 cards fit and a 7th was cut). Now, when the cards do not fit in one row, the extra ones go into a second row above, and a third if needed.",
            "The first cards stay where they are on the bottom row; the newest apps go on top. How many fit per row follows the screen's width (each card is 260 points wide; 6 per row on a 16-inch MacBook Pro) and changes by itself when you connect or remove a screen.",
            "New: weeks in a row. The app counts how many weeks in a row you learned at least one shortcut. A week is Monday to Sunday. The current week does not break the run while it still has no tick: the run only ends when a full week passes with nothing learned.",
            "Weeks in a row show from 2 weeks up. The menu bar menu line now reads \"Learned this week: 3 · 2 weeks in a row\" (before: \"Learned this week: 3\"), and the top line of the Learned page reads \"This week: 3 learned · 12 learned in all · 2 weeks in a row\".",
            "\"Bring back\" on the Learned page (back to still learning) takes that shortcut out of the counts, as before.",
            "Two more ready-made apps: Mail (pink) and Notes (amber), 8 checked shortcuts each, all confirmed on Apple's own pages. Now there are 8 ready-made apps (before: 6). They are not added by themselves: they wait in \"Add ready-made app\" on the Shortcuts page, like Safari. A new installation still starts with the same 5 cards (40 shortcuts), and the button is greyed out only when all 8 are on your list.",
            "All 48 ready-made keys of the 6 earlier apps were checked again. None was wrong, so no key changed. 37 are confirmed on the makers' own pages (Raycast 8, Fusion 8, Safari 8, Finder 7, Xcode 6). The other 11 are the usual keys but stand on no official page: all 8 of Obsidian (its help pages list no keys), Xcode's \"Clean build folder\" and \"Show or hide navigator\", and Finder's \"Show or hide hidden files\". If one of them differs on your Mac, correct it with \"Change\".",
            "Your list is safer. Before, there was one safety copy: the list before your last change. After two changes following a mistake, the old list was in neither file. Now, in addition, the app keeps one dated copy per day, the list as it was before that day's first change, for 30 days, in a folder \"Safety copies\" next to your list (Settings, \"Show in Finder\"). File names look like shortcuts-2026-11-16.json. Copies older than 30 days are cleared away.",
            "A damaged list is now caught. Before, a list file that read as \"no apps at all\" was accepted as your list and later overwrote the good one. Now such a list counts as damaged (the app only ever hides things, so a real list is never empty): the app puts that file aside (shortcuts-unreadable-….json, never overwritten) and starts from the safety copy, or, if that is no good, from the newest good dated copy.",
            "The window shows an amber line saying where your list was restored from. The app also refuses to save an empty list over a good one.",
            "Failed saves are no longer silent. Before, if saving failed, nothing told you. Now a red line \"Could not save your list\" appears at the top of the window and in the menu bar menu. The app tries again every hour and with your next change, and the line goes away when it worked.",
            "More tests: 21 tests (before: 7), including tests for saving, loading and the safety copies. They now run automatically on GitHub after every change to the code (free, because the code is public).",
            "The How it works guide has a new section \"Removed — nothing is ever deleted\", and the texts about the cards, learning this week, adding and changing shortcuts, ready-made apps, the window and \"Your list is safe\" are updated.",
            "Nothing else changed: the learning rules, \"Today's shortcuts\", \"Quiz me\" and your settings work as before.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.3.2 (5)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "Fixed: the keyboard-key icon in the menu bar could not be found. Before, the icon had no saved place, so on every start the Mac put it at the far left of the menu bar icons. That is the side AMS Tidy Bar hides, and on a full bar it sat behind the camera notch even with the drawer open. Now the icon has its own saved place, between AMS Mic Check and AMS Deck Mate, on the side that always shows.",
            "If you move the icon yourself (hold ⌘ and drag it), the app now remembers the new place across restarts. Before, it forgot it.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.3.1 (4)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "Clearer words: ticking a shortcut never deletes it. Before, the app said a shortcut you ticked on its recheck was \"gone for good\" and the Learned page said \"Learned for good\", which sounded as if it was deleted. It never was. Now the Learned page says \"Learned, no more rechecks\", the tooltip on a recheck circle says \"Still know it? Tick to stop the rechecks. It stays in your list.\", and How it works explains that a ticked shortcut is only hidden from the card and stays in your list, with \"Bring back\" at any time.",
            "Nothing else changed: your list, your shortcuts and the learning rules are exactly as before. Only \"Delete\" and \"Remove\" delete, as before.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.3 (3)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "New: \"Learned this week\". The menu of the key icon in the menu bar has a new grey line you cannot click, \"Learned this week: N\", just above the Version line. Before, the menu showed no count of your progress; now it shows how many shortcuts you first ticked as learned since Monday.",
            "Learned this week: the week starts on Monday (Swedish calendar) and starts again at 0 each Monday. A shortcut counts for the week of its first tick, whatever happened afterwards: a later tick at the recheck still counts, and \"Bring back\" removes it from the count because its learned date is cleared.",
            "The Learned page in the window has a new line at the top: \"This week: N learned · M learned in all\". N is the same number as in the menu; M is everything that is on the Learned page right now. Before, the page started straight with the list.",
            "More ready-made apps: the app now carries ready-made lists of 8 checked shortcuts each for 6 apps (before: 3 apps, Obsidian, Raycast and Fusion). New are Finder (blue), Xcode (amber) and Safari (green). Obsidian stays purple, Raycast coral and Fusion teal.",
            "New cards for Finder and Xcode: a fresh installation now starts with 5 cards (before: 3), Obsidian, Raycast, Fusion, Finder and Xcode, 40 shortcuts in all (before: 24). Safari is not added by itself.",
            "If you already used the app, Finder and Xcode are added to your list once, the first time version 0.3 starts, 16 more shortcuts (3 cards become 5). This happens only if no app of that name is on your list yet, and nothing else in your list changes: your shortcuts, ticks, colours and order stay as they are.",
            "New button \"Add ready-made app\" at the bottom of the Shortcuts page, next to \"Add shortcut\". It opens a menu of the ready-made apps that are not on your list yet. Pick one and its card is added with its 8 shortcuts in its own colour, or in the next free colour if that colour is taken. If all 6 are on your list, the button is greyed out. An app whose name is already on the list (in any capitals) is never added twice.",
            "New button \"Remove\" in each app's heading on the Shortcuts page, next to \"Move left\" and \"Move right\". It asks first, for example \"Remove Finder and its 8 shortcuts?\", with Cancel and Remove. Removing deletes the card and all its shortcuts, including the ones you have learned. Before, an app could only be emptied by deleting its shortcuts one by one.",
            "A ready-made app that you removed can be added again; it comes back fresh, with nothing learned. The safety copy of your previous list is kept before every change, as always.",
            "The How it works guide has a new section \"Ready-made apps\" and a new section \"Learned this week\", and the texts about the cards, the window, the menu and \"Your list is safe\" are updated.",
            "Nothing else changed: the learning rules, \"Today's shortcuts\", \"Quiz me\" and your settings work as before.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.2 (2)", date: "16 Nov 2026", items: [
            "New: \"Today's shortcuts\". Instead of showing an app's shortcuts in the order of your list, each card shows a fresh pick every day. The order is shuffled once per day: the same all day, a new one after midnight. The app checks the clock every hour and when the Mac wakes up.",
            "Today's shortcuts: rechecks still come first. The card shows as many shortcuts as \"Shortcuts per card\" says; when you tick one off, the next of today's pick moves up. The card's header says \"Today\" next to the app's name. Off by default.",
            "New: \"Quiz me\". The keys on the cards are hidden: each key shows as a blank key cap with a \"?\", so you still see how many keys it takes. Point at a row with the mouse, or click the hidden keys, and the real keys appear. Move away and they hide again; a click keeps them shown until you click again. Try to remember the keys before you look. Off by default.",
